#. Tag: title
#: project-management.docbook:11
#, no-c-format
msgid "Building and Project Management"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: project-management.docbook:13
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"This chapter deals only with compiled projects, such as C++, &Java; or Fortran "
"projects. Projects for scripting languages like Python and PHP work very "
"differently."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: project-management.docbook:19
#, no-c-format
msgid "You will find here information on:"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: project-management.docbook:22
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"<link linkend=\"automake-manager-summary\">Summary of &automanag;</link> "
"containing an initial overall view of &automanag;,"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: project-management.docbook:25
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"<link linkend=\"automake-manager-operation\">Automake Manager Operation</link> "
"describing the basics of how to work with &automanag;,"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: project-management.docbook:31
#, no-c-format
msgid "Summary of &automanag;"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: project-management.docbook:33
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"In the <link linkend=\"unixdev-buildsystems\">Build systems</link> "
"chapter we have given a rough overview of the build systems commonly in use on "
"&UNIX; systems. In the following sections we will look at this in more detail."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: project-management.docbook:36
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"There is some confusion about how to name such things. &GNU; calls them <quote>"
"build systems</quote> when it describes Automake, Autoconf and Libtool. QMake "
"calls itself <quote>a tool to write Makefiles for different compilers and "
"platforms</quote>. In &kde; often the term <quote>project management "
"systems</quote> is used. We will use this term in a broader sense to describe "
"the built-in environments in &kdevelop; which are used to organize and build "
"your projects. In the context of this section, however, we will mostly talk "
"about <quote>automated build systems</quote>."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: project-management.docbook:41
#, no-c-format
msgid "The Need for an Automated Build System"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: project-management.docbook:43
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"If you have a simple <quote>Hello World</quote> program, written in C, you can "
"compile and link it using &gcc; <parameter>-o hello hello.c</parameter> "
"and execute it using the command <command>./hello</command>"
", so you do not even need a <filename>Makefile</filename>."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: project-management.docbook:46
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"If you have a C application with several modules and header files and you are "
"only going to run it on your own machine (&ie; it is an in-house application), "
"you will only need a simple <filename>Makefile</filename>"
", which is fairly easy to write by hand (use <userinput>info make</userinput> "
"to find out more)."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: project-management.docbook:49
#, no-c-format
msgid "The complications begin when:"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: project-management.docbook:53
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Your source-code, documentation, graphics, sounds, translations, data files, "
"&etc; are located in more than one directory,"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: project-management.docbook:56
#, no-c-format
msgid "You have a hierarchy of directories and sub-directories,"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: project-management.docbook:59
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"You are using libraries that are not part of the traditional &UNIX; set, such "
"as the &Qt; Object Library or the &kde; Desktop libraries,"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: project-management.docbook:62
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"You are using a pre-processor to generate some of your source-code, such as "
"Qt's MOC pre-compiler,"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: project-management.docbook:65
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"You aim to distribute your application worldwide, to people who may not have "
"the same &UNIX;/&Linux; system, software and hardware as you,"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: project-management.docbook:68
#, no-c-format
msgid "You require an automated Install and Uninstall facility,"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: project-management.docbook:71
#, no-c-format
msgid "You aim to make your application part of the &kde; Desktop set."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: project-management.docbook:76
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"If you have some or all of the above situations, you probably need a <emphasis>"
"build system</emphasis>. In the example above we used &gcc; to compile and "
"build the <quote>Hello World</quote> program, but not all C compilers are "
"called <quote>&gcc;</quote>. So if you distribute your application to someone "
"who is using some other C compiler, your Makefile must somehow use the name of "
"that person's compiler, otherwise your application will fail to "
"compile&mdash;and that is just simple example of what can go wrong."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: project-management.docbook:79
#, no-c-format
msgid "A build system will iron out these differences for you."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: project-management.docbook:83
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"It will check that the libraries you need are present on each receiving "
"machine,"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: project-management.docbook:86
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"will automatically scan all your application directories for files to "
"pre-process, compile or install and"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: project-management.docbook:89
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"will install the components of your application in the correct receiving "
"directories, making sure that"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: project-management.docbook:92
#, no-c-format
msgid "the directories are created in the receiving machine as required."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: project-management.docbook:97
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"In brief, a build system offers safe and secure methods for your application to "
"be compiled and installed correctly on any receiving machine. As we have shown "
"before in the <link linkend=\"makesystems\">Project Management Systems</link> "
"survey, &kdevelop; offers three automated build systems and the option of "
"creating your own Makefile, in short (click on the project names to get more "
"information):"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: project-management.docbook:101
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"<link linkend=\"automake-project\">Automake projects</link> "
"which use the &GNU; standard development tools."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: project-management.docbook:104
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"<link linkend=\"qmake-project\">QMake projects</link> "
"which use the trolltech QMake project manager."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: project-management.docbook:107
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"<link linkend=\"ant-project\">ANT projects</link> which use the Apache ANT "
"project manager for &Java; development."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: project-management.docbook:110
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"<link linkend=\"custom-project\">Custom projects</link> "
"which require you to maintain your own <filename>Makefiles</filename>."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: project-management.docbook:116
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"One of these four alternatives must be chosen when you create a project and "
"<emphasis>the choice is difficult to change later</emphasis>"
", so you should give it some thought before you start."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: project-management.docbook:124
#, no-c-format
msgid "Tutorials on Autoconf/Automake/Libtool"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: project-management.docbook:125
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"There are several tutorials available on the &GNU; Build System (<command>"
"Autoconf</command>, <command>Automake</command> and <command>Libtool</command>"
") of which the &automanag; makes use."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: project-management.docbook:129
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"A short <ulink "
"url=\"http://www.kdevelop.org/index.html?filename=tutorial_autoconf.html\">"
"autoconf tutorial</ulink> written by Christopher W. Curtis available on the "
"&kdevelop; home page. It concentrates on some basic steps to modify a <filename>"
"Makefile</filename>."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: project-management.docbook:131
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"A more detailed tutorial can be found in a greater set of tutorials on <ulink "
"url=\"http://www.amath.washington.edu/~lf/tutorials/autoconf/toolsmanual_toc.htm"
"l\"><quote>Developing software with GNU</quote></ulink>."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: project-management.docbook:134
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"And there is the famous <ulink url=\"http://sources.redhat.com/autobook\">"
"<quote>Goat Book</quote></ulink>, titled <quote>Autoconf, Automake, and "
"Libtool</quote>. This is an easily readable, yet concise, introduction in all "
"main aspects of the &GNU; Autotools."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: project-management.docbook:142
#, no-c-format
msgid "What does &automanag; Do?"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: project-management.docbook:144
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"The <link linkend=\"applicationwizard\">&appwizard;</link> "
"will have set up some initial <filename>Makefile.am</filename> "
"files when you created a <guilabel>New Project</guilabel> "
"of a type that uses the &GNU; Build System, such as <menuchoice><guimenuitem>"
"C++</guimenuitem> <guimenuitem>KDE</guimenuitem> <guimenuitem>"
"Application framework</guimenuitem></menuchoice>. During development "
"&automanag; creates any other <filename>Makefile.am</filename> "
"files for projects that use the &GNU; Build System and maintains them all, "
"&appwizard; and &automanag; created alike."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: project-management.docbook:147
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"There will be one <filename>Makefile.am</filename> file in each directory of "
"your project that contains files to be compiled or installed. It will contain "
"your specifications for compiling, building and installing files and a "
"reference to any subdirectories that also have a <filename>"
"Makefile.am</filename> file and possibly some files to compile, build and "
"install."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: project-management.docbook:151
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Your project's directories and source files may be structured to any depth, or "
"you may prefer a flat project-structure with all subdirectories at the top "
"level."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: project-management.docbook:155
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"The aim of the &GNU; Build System is to produce source-code file structures "
"that can be compiled, built and installed on any &UNIX; or &Linux; system by "
"using the simple commands:"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: screen
#: project-management.docbook:159
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"./configure\n"
"make\n"
"make install    # Usually as \"root\"."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: project-management.docbook:161
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"and can be uninstalled by the command <command>make uninstall</command> "
"(usually as root)."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: project-management.docbook:164
#, no-c-format
msgid "How does this work? Well <command>configure</command> is a script that"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: project-management.docbook:168
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"works out the details of whatever system it is in, such as what compiler and "
"libraries to use and where they are located, and then"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: project-management.docbook:171
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"creates recursive <filename>Makefile</filename> files by filling in the "
"substitutions in the corresponding <filename>Makefile.in</filename> files."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: project-management.docbook:176
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"The <filename>Makefile.in</filename> are <quote>input</quote> "
"files&mdash;templates which provide basic information for the <filename>"
"Makefile</filename>s to be produced from them by filling in some system "
"dependent information. They are generated by the <command>Automake</command> "
"utility from the <filename>Makefile.am</filename> files."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: project-management.docbook:179
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"The process of going from <filename>Makefile.am</filename> (<filename>"
".am</filename> denotes <quote>Automake</quote> template files) to <filename>"
"Makefile</filename> files is handled automatically by the &kdevelop; "
"&promanag;, using the <command>Autoconf</command> utility, <command>M4</command> "
"macros and other arcana we need not go into here."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: project-management.docbook:182
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"So when <command>make</command> runs, it automatically picks up the correct "
"pieces from the current environment, such as compilers and libraries. "
"Similarly, <command>make install</command> puts your application components, "
"such as executables, documentation and data files in the correct places for "
"that environment."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: project-management.docbook:186
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"If you distribute your application as a <quote>tarball</quote> "
"(a single compressed file that &kdevelop; can create for you), it will include "
"the <filename>Makefile.in</filename> files and the <filename>"
"configure</filename> script file, so the recipient can compile, build and "
"install your application without having <command>Automake</command>, <command>"
"Autoconf</command> or &kdevelop; on their machine. The <filename>"
"Makefile.am</filename> files are also included, just in case the receiver needs "
"to do any source-code modifications."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: project-management.docbook:189
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"The rules are rather different if you distribute via a web-based source-code "
"repository such as &kde; &cvs;."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: project-management.docbook:196
#, no-c-format
msgid "Summary of What Automake Manager Does"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: project-management.docbook:199
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Generates <filename>Makefile.am</filename> files in subdirectories it knows as "
"<quote>subprojects</quote>."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: project-management.docbook:202
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Updates <filename>Makefile.am</filename> files as the project structure "
"changes."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: project-management.docbook:205
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Updates <filename>Makefile.am</filename> files as files are added to or removed "
"from the project."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: project-management.docbook:208
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Accepts definitions of how the various files are to be built or installed and "
"modifies the <filename>Makefile.am</filename> accordingly."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: project-management.docbook:211
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Accepts parameters used in building or installing (&eg; library names) and "
"ensures that they are used in the required compilation and build steps."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: project-management.docbook:219
#, no-c-format
msgid "Contents of Automake Files"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: project-management.docbook:221
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"A <filename>Makefile.am</filename> file has lines containing variable-names "
"followed by an equals sign and a list of files or parameter values. The <quote>"
"variables</quote> have two-part names, such as <varname>bin_PROGRAMS</varname>"
", <varname>myapp_SOURCES</varname> or <varname>kdelnk_DATA</varname>"
". The second part is called the <emphasis>primary</emphasis> "
"and represents something from which to build or install. The first part is "
"called the <emphasis>prefix</emphasis> and represents:"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: project-management.docbook:225
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"A <emphasis>directory</emphasis> in which to do installation (&eg; <filename>"
"bin</filename>),"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: project-management.docbook:228
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"A <emphasis>qualifier</emphasis> for the primary (&eg; <varname>myapp</varname> "
"for <varname>SOURCES</varname>, indicating that the source files listed after "
"<varname>myapp_SOURCES</varname> go into building <filename>myapp</filename>),"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: project-management.docbook:231
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"A special <emphasis>prefix</emphasis> <varname>noinst</varname> "
"(short for <quote>no installation</quote>), usually used to list program header "
"files (<filename>.h</filename>),"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: project-management.docbook:234
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Or the special prefix <varname>EXTRA</varname>, for <emphasis>"
"configuration-dependent</emphasis> stuff."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: project-management.docbook:239
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"For more information on <command>Automake</command> and <filename>"
"Makefile.am</filename> files, look up <userinput>info Automake</userinput>."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: project-management.docbook:242
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Basically, &automanag; creates and updates the variable-names and lists of "
"files or parameters. See the following example of a <filename>"
"Makefile.am</filename> for a typical application, called <filename>"
"myapp</filename>."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: screen
#: project-management.docbook:246
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"## Makefile.am for myapp\n"
"\n"
"# this is the program that gets installed.  its name is used for all\n"
"# of the other Makefile.am variables\n"
"bin_PROGRAMS = myapp\n"
"\n"
"# set the include path for X, qt and KDE\n"
"INCLUDES = $(all_includes)\n"
"\n"
"# the library search path.\n"
"myapp_LDFLAGS = $(KDE_RPATH) $(all_libraries)\n"
"\n"
"# the libraries to link against.\n"
"myapp_LDADD   = $(LIB_KFILE) $(LIB_KDEPRINT)\n"
"\n"
"# which sources should be compiled for myapp\n"
"myapp_SOURCES = main.cpp myapp.cpp myappview.cpp\n"
"\n"
"# these are the headers for your project\n"
"noinst_HEADERS = myapp.h myappview.h\n"
"\n"
"# let automoc handle all of the meta source files (moc)\n"
"METASOURCES = AUTO\n"
"\n"
"KDE_ICON = myapp\n"
"\n"
"# this is where the kdelnk file will go\n"
"kdelnkdir = $(kde_appsdir)/Utilities\n"
"kdelnk_DATA = myapp.desktop\n"
"\n"
"# this is where the XML-GUI resource file goes\n"
"rcdir = $(kde_datadir)/myapp\n"
"rc_DATA = myappui.rc\n"
"\n"
"AM_CXXFLAGS = -DMY_C++_PREPROCESSOR_OPTION"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: project-management.docbook:248
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"As you can see, many of the items on the right hand side are symbols of the "
"form <varname>$(xxx)</varname>. These are <emphasis>"
"environment variables</emphasis> which are defined in the actual &kde; "
"environment and are substituted with real values when <command>"
"./configure</command> generates the final <filename>Makefile</filename> "
"files in the receiving machine."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: project-management.docbook:252
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Also, sometime after you have started with &kdevelop;, it is a good idea to run "
"the command <command>./configure --help</command>, which will show you the "
"range of things you can change at build and installation time, such as for a "
"test environment. In particular, the command: <screen>\n"
"./configure --prefix=/where/you/wish\n"
"</screen> will re-direct the entire installation to a directory structure of "
"your choice, by changing the internal variable <varname>$(prefix)</varname> "
"to value <filename class=\"directory\">/where/you/wish</filename>."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: project-management.docbook:266
#, no-c-format
msgid "Automake Manager Operation"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: project-management.docbook:268
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"In this chapter you will find a basic description of the &automanag; elements "
"and how to use them. This covers:"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: project-management.docbook:271
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"<link linkend=\"automake-manager-operation-window\">"
"The &automanag; Window</link> describes the basic structure of the &automanag; "
"main window."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: project-management.docbook:274
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"<link linkend=\"automake-manager-operation-survey-window\">"
"The Overall View Window</link> describes the elements of the upper subwindow."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: project-management.docbook:277
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"<link linkend=\"automake-manager-operation-detail-window\">"
"The Detail View Window</link> describes the elements of the lower subwindow."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: project-management.docbook:280
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"<link linkend=\"automake-manager-operation-navigate\">"
"Navigating in the &automanag;</link> lists some basic operations you can "
"perform in the &automanag;."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: project-management.docbook:283
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"<link linkend=\"automake-manager-operation-popups\">"
"Popup Menus in the &automanag;</link> describes the windows which will pop up "
"when you select an action in the &automanag;."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: project-management.docbook:290
#, no-c-format
msgid "The &automanag; Window"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: project-management.docbook:300
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"&automanag; runs in a split window. The top part is called the <emphasis>"
"Overall View</emphasis> and the bottom part is called the <emphasis>"
"Detail View</emphasis>. Between them is a narrow bar that can be dragged with "
"the mouse to adjust the sizes of the views. In IDEAl mode you can also drag the "
"side of the split window to change the width."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: project-management.docbook:303
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"On top of each view there is a <emphasis>toolbar</emphasis>"
", the buttons in which will become activated when an element in this view is "
"selected. This provides one way you can access the actions provided for that "
"view element. The other are context menus which pop up on right mouse button "
"click as will be discussed below."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: project-management.docbook:306
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"In IDEAl mode there are two additional small buttons in the &automanag; window "
"titlebar left hand side &ndash; a triangular shaped right arrow, and a dot "
"button. The arrow button is used to <emphasis>close the window</emphasis>"
". The dot button on the other hand will <emphasis>keep the window "
"open</emphasis> even if another &kdevelop; window has been selected. (Otherwise "
"the &automanag; window will automatically close whenever another window gets "
"the input focus.)"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: project-management.docbook:316
#, no-c-format
msgid "The Overall View Window"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: project-management.docbook:318
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"The overall view window contains a tree-list of all the directories in your "
"project that contain program files, documentation or data. Each such directory "
"contains a <filename>Makefile.am</filename> file and is known in &automanag; as "
"a <emphasis>subproject</emphasis>. There are three typical subprojects in a "
"&kde;-based project as shown in the above illustration:"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: project-management.docbook:323
#, no-c-format
msgid "<filename>src</filename> &ndash; source-code files for your application,"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: project-management.docbook:326
#, no-c-format
msgid "<filename>doc</filename> &ndash; your user manual or Handbook,"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: project-management.docbook:329
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"<filename>po</filename> &ndash; extracts of strings in your source-code files "
"that require translation into other human languages (&eg; window titles, menu "
"names, button labels, dialog box text and messages of various kinds)."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: project-management.docbook:334
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Note that the <filename>doc</filename> subproject always has an <filename>"
"en</filename> subproject, which you can see if you click on the <symbol>"
"+</symbol> symbol next to the word <filename>doc</filename>"
". That is because the base language of all documentation in &kde; is United "
"States English (en). If your application becomes part of &kde;, the &kde; "
"translation teams may translate your documentation from United States English "
"into other languages and the translations will go into other subprojects, such "
"as <filename>de</filename> (German) or <filename>fr</filename> "
"(French). The strings in the <filename>po</filename> "
"subproject may also be translated and stored in other files in <filename>"
"po</filename>, thus allowing your application to be operated by people who do "
"not know English."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: project-management.docbook:338
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"The <filename>doc</filename> and <filename>po</filename> "
"subprojects serve different purposes. <filename>doc</filename> "
"contains <emphasis>documentation</emphasis> like a user manual, <filename>"
"po</filename> contains translatable text strings of the <emphasis>"
"user interface</emphasis> which is integrated in the source code of this "
"application."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: project-management.docbook:342
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"The overall view window serves&mdash;amongst other things&mdash;as a navigation "
"tool. If you select a subproject in the overall view window, the corresponding "
"details will be shown in the detail view window."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: project-management.docbook:349
#, no-c-format
msgid "The Detail View Window"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: project-management.docbook:351
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"The detail view contains a tree-list of all the files in the subproject "
"currently selected in the overall view as well as the compilation, build and "
"installation rules for this subproject. Thus the two views together can give "
"you access to all the components of your application and all the information on "
"how to compile, build and install it."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: project-management.docbook:356
#, no-c-format
msgid "Targets"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: project-management.docbook:358
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"The tree-list in the detail view has two levels. The top level consists of "
"so-called &automanag; <emphasis>targets</emphasis> and the next level contains "
"lists of files that go to make up each target."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: project-management.docbook:361
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"This concept of an &automanag; target differs somewhat from what a <filename>"
"Makefile</filename> target usually is. In short:"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: project-management.docbook:364
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"The definition of how a set of files is to be compiled, built or installed is "
"known as a <emphasis>target</emphasis> in &automanag;, but as a <emphasis>"
"variable</emphasis> in <command>Automake</command> itself."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: project-management.docbook:367
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"A <emphasis>target</emphasis> in <command>make</command> "
"is often something quite different, being the parameter of a <command>"
"make</command> command (&eg; <userinput>make install</userinput>, <userinput>"
"make clean</userinput>)."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: project-management.docbook:370
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"However some <filename>Makefile.am</filename> variables do represent an "
"underlying <emphasis>sub-target</emphasis> in <command>make</command>."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: project-management.docbook:379
#, no-c-format
msgid "Navigating in the &automanag;"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: project-management.docbook:381
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"In both the overall and the detail view you can left-click on the <symbol>"
"+</symbol> or <symbol>-</symbol> next to a subproject or target name to expand "
"or contract the tree view. If you do that with a <emphasis>subproject</emphasis> "
"in the overall view, it shows or hides the subprojects at the next level down "
"(if any). If you do it with a <emphasis>target</emphasis> "
"in the detail view, it shows or hides the list of files that go into that "
"target."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: term
#: project-management.docbook:387
#, no-c-format
msgid "Opening a file for Edit"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: project-management.docbook:389
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"If you <emphasis>&LMB; click</emphasis> on a file name in the detail view, the "
"corresponding file opens up in &kdevelop;'s editing window."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: term
#: project-management.docbook:395
#, no-c-format
msgid "Activating the &automanag; Toolbar Buttons"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: project-management.docbook:397
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"If you <emphasis>&LMB; click</emphasis> on the name of a subproject in the "
"overall view or target in the detail view, the name is highlighted and some "
"toolbar buttons become active in the top part of that view."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: project-management.docbook:403
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"It is recommended that you use the <emphasis>right mouse-button and popup "
"menus</emphasis>, rather than the toolbar buttons, because it is then much "
"easier to see and understand what you are doing."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: project-management.docbook:406
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Operations on subprojects and targets have far-reaching effects on the "
"structure, compilation, building and installation of your application."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: term
#: project-management.docbook:414
#, no-c-format
msgid "Selecting Actions/Popup Menus"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: project-management.docbook:416
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"If you <emphasis>&RMB; click</emphasis> on the name of a subproject, target or "
"file, a menu pops up and you can then select actions to perform on the "
"subproject, target or file, such as add a target to the subproject, add a file "
"to a target or logically remove the selected file from its target."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: project-management.docbook:426
#, no-c-format
msgid "Popup Menus in the &automanag;"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: project-management.docbook:428
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"The following sections explain in short terms which operations the menus make "
"available which will pop up on right mouse button clicks in the &automanag; "
"window. They are meant for overall view only. You will find detailed "
"descriptions of most operations in a later chapter."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: project-management.docbook:434
#, no-c-format
msgid "The Popup Menu for a File"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: project-management.docbook:436
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"When you &RMB; click on a <emphasis>file name</emphasis> "
"in the detail view the following menu will pop up allowing you to select one of "
"several operations to be performed on that file. In the illustration below the "
"<filename>hi-16app-myapp.png</filename> icon file was selected from the "
"<guilabel>Icon data in myapp</guilabel> target of the <guilabel>"
"myapp/src</guilabel> subproject."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: project-management.docbook:448
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"The main popup-menu item for a file is to <guimenuitem>Remove</guimenuitem> "
"the file from its target (&ie; it will no longer be used to compile, build or "
"install that target)."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: project-management.docbook:451
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"The <guimenuitem>CVS</guimenuitem> item offers a variety of CVS operations on "
"the file."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: project-management.docbook:454
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"The <guimenuitem>Open With</guimenuitem> item allows you to open the file with "
"a variety of editors or with any application at all (&eg; you can open the icon "
"file in our example with <application>K3Icon</application>)."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: project-management.docbook:457
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"The <guimenuitem>Perforce</guimenuitem> item is used for similar operations as "
"in CVS using the commercial <quote><application>Perforce</application></quote> "
"version control system."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: project-management.docbook:467
#, no-c-format
msgid "The Popup Menu for a Target"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: project-management.docbook:469
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"When you right-click on a <emphasis>target</emphasis> "
"in the detail view the following menu will pop up allowing you to select one of "
"several operations to be performed on it. In the illustration below the "
"<guilabel>myapp (Program in bin)</guilabel> target of the <guilabel>"
"myapp/src</guilabel> subproject was selected."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: project-management.docbook:481
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"The <guimenuitem>Options</guimenuitem> item for a target only applies to source "
"code files. In the corresponding dialog box you can specify linker flags and "
"paths on which to locate libraries and you can give a list of actual libraries "
"to be linked in to your application."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: project-management.docbook:484
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"The <guimenuitem>Create New File</guimenuitem> item brings up a dialog in which "
"you can set the file name and the type of file to be generated (from a "
"drop-down list)."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: project-management.docbook:487
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"The <guimenuitem>Add Existing Files</guimenuitem> item brings up a dialog box "
"in which you can add an already existing file to this target."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: project-management.docbook:490
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"The <guimenuitem>Remove</guimenuitem> item for a target allows you to logically "
"remove the target and all its files from the project structure."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: project-management.docbook:493
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"The <guimenuitem>Make Target Active</guimenuitem> item only applies to targets "
"containing source code files. New files will always be added to such an active "
"target."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: project-management.docbook:496
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"The <guimenuitem>Build Target</guimenuitem> item calls all necessary compile "
"and make operations to build the code for this target only."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: project-management.docbook:506
#, no-c-format
msgid "The Popup Menu for a Subproject"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: project-management.docbook:508
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"When you &RMB; click on a <emphasis>subproject</emphasis> "
"in the overall view window the following menu will pop up which allows you to "
"make major changes to the structure of your project and the way it is compiled, "
"built and installed. You can use it to expand or modify the basic project "
"structure that the &appwizard; has created."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: project-management.docbook:521
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"The <guimenuitem>Options</guimenuitem> item for a subproject controls the way "
"that the subproject will be compiled, built and installed. The dialog box that "
"pops up has tabs for Compiler, Includes, Prefixes and Build Order."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: project-management.docbook:524
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"The <guimenuitem>Add Subproject</guimenuitem> item creates a new directory and "
"skeleton Makefile.am file."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: project-management.docbook:527
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"The <guimenuitem>Add Target</guimenuitem> item pops up a dialog in which you "
"can set the rules for compiling, building or installing a group of files within "
"your subproject."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: project-management.docbook:530
#, no-c-format
msgid "<guimenuitem>Add Service</guimenuitem> (... to be written ...)"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: project-management.docbook:533
#, no-c-format
msgid "<guimenuitem>Add Application</guimenuitem> (... to be written ...)"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: project-management.docbook:536
#, no-c-format
msgid "<guimenuitem>Add Existing Subprojects</guimenuitem> (... to be written ...)"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: project-management.docbook:539
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"The <guimenuitem>Remove Subproject</guimenuitem> item in the popup menu for a "
"subproject is the proper way to remove a subproject. It will adjust the "
"<filename>Makefile.am</filename> files accordingly. You will also be offered "
"the option to delete all the files (or links) in the corresponding "
"subdirectory. Obviously, this feature should be used with caution."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: project-management.docbook:542
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"The <guimenuitem>Build</guimenuitem> item calls all necessary compile and make "
"operations to build the code for this subproject only."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: project-management.docbook:545
#, no-c-format
msgid "<guimenuitem>Force Reedit</guimenuitem> (... to be written ...)"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: project-management.docbook:548
#, no-c-format
msgid "<guimenuitem>Clean</guimenuitem> (... to be written ...)"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: project-management.docbook:551
#, no-c-format
msgid "<guimenuitem>Install</guimenuitem> (... to be written ...)"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: project-management.docbook:554
#, no-c-format
msgid "<guimenuitem>Install (as root user)</guimenuitem> (... to be written ...)"
msgstr ""

